{
  "evidence": "TEXT_F: Stronger terror controls, mandatory deradicalisation, overhaul Prevent; protect neighbourhood policing, safeguard 10,000 officers, create a Police Standards Authority and chartered officers; recruit 1,000 extra border staff, full exit checks, retain cap on non‑EU workers, require English for public‑facing roles, two‑year wait for in‑work benefits for new EU arrivals; maintain a minimum, credible independent nuclear deterrent and strengthen the Armed Forces Covenant; cut the deficit every year with OBR audits, cap structural social security spending, keep the benefit cap, higher contributory JSA, restrict Winter Fuel Payments to the richest 5%; worker protections (ban exploitative zero‑hours), lower tuition fees, renters’ rights, gender pay‑gap reporting, race‑equality strategy, advance LGBT rights. TEXT_G: Oppose blanket surveillance, presumption against short prison terms, diversion for drug possession and end imprisonment for personal possession, allow medicinal cannabis; reduce nuclear patrol posture; protect human rights via a Digital Bill of Rights; balance the structural current budget by 2017/18, temporary 1% cap on working‑age benefit uprating, sanctions ‘yellow card’, maintain pensions triple lock; faster asylum decisions and allow work after 6 months; proportional voting and elected second chamber; remain engaged in the EU.",
  "reasoning": "With Authority and Loyalty weighted highest in the profile, TEXT_F aligns better by prioritizing stronger policing, counter‑terror powers, border enforcement, an English requirement for public‑facing roles, and maintaining the nuclear deterrent, all of which emphasize order, hierarchy, and in‑group protection. Both texts reflect proportionality and equality, but TEXT_G’s emphasis on civil liberties, decriminalization, and reduced nuclear posture is less consistent with high Authority/Loyalty priorities, making TEXT_F the closer overall fit.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_F"
}
